{# version: 2 #}
Build the notation glossary of a research paper: the mathematical symbols the method uses,
with what each denotes. Use only the text and equations below. Every paragraph starts with an
id in brackets.

For each symbol return:
- "symbol": the symbol as it appears in the equations' LaTeX (e.g. "d_k", "\alpha",
  "W^Q_i", "\mathcal{L}"), or as written in the text if there is no LaTeX.
- "meaning": what it denotes, in a few words ("dimension of the keys").
- "quote": ONE sentence copied character-for-character from the Text section below (not from
  the equations) that defines or explains it. Copy it as plain text exactly as it appears
  there: no LaTeX, no "$" signs, even where the sentence contains math.
- "chunk_id": the id in brackets of the paragraph the quote comes from.

Return at most 30 symbols: the ones central to the method first. Skip ordinary function
names (softmax, max, log) unless the paper defines them, and skip indices and constants that
need no explanation. List each symbol once. If the text does not say what a symbol means,
leave it out rather than guessing. Keep every string short.

Equations:
{% for eq in equations -%}
{{ eq.number or "(unnumbered)" }} {{ eq.body }}
{% endfor %}

Text:
{{ text }}
